The Deno binding loads the installed shared `libpancake` over
[Deno's FFI](https://docs.deno.com/runtime/fundamentals/ffi/) and presents a `Peer`
class in
[`bindings/deno/pancake.ts`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/deno/pancake.ts).
Wire values arrive as discriminated unions, so a delivery is a value you switch on
with the type narrowing you already expect from TypeScript.

## Async

The async surface returns native `Promise`s and supports `using`, so a connection
cleans itself up at the end of a block. An `AsyncConnection` runs the peer over
`Deno.connect` and `Deno.listen`. You read events as a stream with
`for await (const event of conn)` and await a result directly with
`await vat.send(ref, verb, args)`: no polling, no callback registration.

## Actors

The actor layer is a `Vat` over `(verb, args) => Argument` callbacks. A local actor
is a plain function; an eventual-send to a partner-hosted reference resolves the
returned promise when the answer settles.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build deno-dogfood    # the Deno consumer against libpancake
```

The full surface lives in the
[Deno binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/deno/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/deno/room_client.ts)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
